    "created_at": "2016-07-06T16:54:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-07-06T16:54:48Z",
    "body": "One question for editors: I consistified a lot of the script fetching algorithms, but one inconsistency that still bothers me is that \"fetch a classic script\" takes a CORS setting, and \"fetch a module script tree\" takes a credentials mode. We could do a few things:\n- Leave as-is; it's fine\n- Change both to use credentials mode (the more modern thing), and manually translate CORS setting attributes to credentials modes before calling \"fetch a classic script\". This would mean abandoning the use of \"create a potential-CORS request\" in \"fetch a classic script\"\n- Change both to use credentials mode, and update \"create a potential-CORS request\" to take a credentials mode, and update all its call-sites to do the translation?\n\n## \n\n> I gave feedback in #1485. Basically, it's not clear to me that the top-level flag is really a thing in practice.\n\nLooking at all the things service workers do, I think at least _some_ of them are top-level only. For example, setting the `Service-Worker: script` header, and disallowing redirects. So I think the flag is going to be necessary, even if it's currently over-applied by the service worker spec.\n\n> Also, are you updating worklets and service workers too to use this new single hook?\n\nI got service workers in https://github.com/slightlyoff/ServiceWorker/pull/923. I can do worklets after that.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
